      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      },
      "strings": {
        "brief": "Welcome to this year's edition. It isn't a lecture, just three things worth knowing this year and then some practice.\n\nScams switch channels. A text, then a call, then an email. Check another way, every time.\n\nA voice isn't proof. A voice note or call from someone you know can be faked. Call back on the number you already have.\n\nYour login is the prize. A prompt you didn't start, a caller who wants a code, a colleague who wants your password: all the same request.\n\nWhen a message pressures you, break the chain at the first link: pause, check another way, report. Now the practice.",
        "defendIntro": "A mixed run of this year's messages, real and fake, across email, calls, a voicemail, a text, a sign-in prompt and a page. The sitting moves you between seats: a payments inbox, then a service-desk queue, then a phone. Take them one at a time, and say why on each.",
        "defendOnly": "These are this year's messages, real ones among the scams, and they were built to catch a busy day. Anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "noBlameLine": "Being caught by one of these is no fault of yours. Testers are professionals, and the year's scams were built to look ordinary. Reporting what you noticed is the win, and reporting your own slip quickly is what the course rewards most.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: break the chain at the first link. A scam that switches from a text to a call to an email is still one attack: pause at the first step, check another way on a number or app you already trust, and report it using the route in the box below. A familiar voice is not proof; ring back on the number you already have."
      }
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
      }
    }
